<Project>

  <!--
    Centralized build settings. Values defined here apply to every project in
    the solution; individual .csproj files only override when they need to
    diverge. Keeps the "best-practice baseline" in one place.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <AnalysisMode>All</AnalysisMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Generate XML documentation for every project, Collatz.Tests and
    Collatz.Experiments included. (We can't gate this on $(IsPackable) here
    because the SDK locks in its decision before Directory.Build.targets runs.)

    Those two used to opt out, and this comment used to say so. Opting out made
    IDE0005 structurally unable to fire in them, because documentation
    generation is that rule's documented prerequisite (roslyn#41640), which is
    the hole halheinrich/Math#43 is about: setting a severity in .editorconfig
    would have left two of this member's three projects unable to report the
    rule at all. An unused using rots identically in a test and in a console
    experiment, so the prerequisite is now met in all three projects rather than
    the rule scoped away from two of them, and each of the two suppresses CS1591
    instead.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Every ProjectReference builds in the configuration its consumer was asked
    for. halheinrich/Math#47.

    This member is the odd one, and the property changes nothing observable
    here today. Collatz.sln is the only solution in the umbrella that lists
    ..\BigRationalLibrary\BigRationalLibrary\BigRationalLibrary.csproj, so the
    producer has an entry in this member's solution map and the escaping
    ProjectReference in Collatz.csproj already resolved to Release in a Release
    build. That is why the defect was found in the other two members and never
    in this one, and this comment should not be read as claiming it fixed
    anything.

    It is adopted for two reasons. The baseline is one thing in every member
    rather than a property three repos carry and one omits. And it is what
    makes dropping the producer from this member's solution safe: membership is
    the only thing holding this edge right today, and the Conventions section
    of the umbrella's VISION.md says a solution's membership must not be
    load-bearing.

    Where the default is wrong, and the citation. In
    Microsoft.Common.CurrentVersion.targets on SDK 10.0.400, line 1584 defaults
    ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform to true whenever
    BuildingSolutionFile or BuildingInsideVisualStudio is set, and line 1609
    hands it to the AssignProjectConfiguration task, whose job is then to
    rewrite SetConfiguration on every reference absent from the solution map,
    unsetting the parent's configuration so the referenced project falls back
    to its own default, which is Debug. Line 1584's condition names
    BuildingInsideVisualStudio as well, so the defect was never confined to
    `dotnet build <solution>`: in the members that had it, Release builds
    inside Visual Studio 2026 linked the Debug producer too.

    How to re-establish that this holds, since the failure mode is silent and
    a Release build that links a Debug producer reports 0 warnings and 0
    errors. Build the producer in Release, hash the producer's own Release
    output, hash the copy of it that landed in each of this member's
    bin/Release outputs, and compare, recomputing both sides in the same run so
    that neither is a remembered value. tools/check-release-linkage.sh in the
    umbrella is what does that, and it inspects all three of this member's
    output copies rather than stopping at the first. No producer hash is quoted
    here on purpose (halheinrich/Math#49): the SDK embeds the producer's HEAD
    sha in AssemblyInformationalVersion, so a hash identifies a commit rather
    than a source state and goes stale at the producer's next commit, not at
    its next source change.

    Flagged rather than quietly relied on, on three counts. It overrides an
    MSBuild default instead of working with it. The property is not part of the
    documented public surface; it lives in the SDK's targets, which is where it
    was read, so an SDK upgrade may change it with no error and a silently
    wrong binary as the symptom. And it propagates Platform as well as
    Configuration, which is harmless only while every member is AnyCPU-only.
    The default exists so a solution configuration is not forced onto a project
    that may not define it; every member here defines exactly Debug and Release
    with no platform variation, so the condition it guards against does not
    arise. If either premise changes, this is the decision to revisit.

    Preferred over keeping the producer in this member's solution, which also
    works and uses only supported solution semantics: it is what has been
    working here. Membership puts a foreign project inside a member's solution,
    it scales with dependency depth, it has to be re-established every time the
    graph grows, and when incomplete it fails silently, restoring exactly this
    defect with exactly this symptom.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform>false</ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Lock-file based version pinning. The lock file (packages.lock.json)
    captures exactly the resolved package versions and is committed to git,
    so any clone restores the same dependency graph. RestoreLockedMode fails
    the restore if the lock file is out of sync with the csproj.

    Unconditional, deliberately. This was gated on ContinuousIntegrationBuild,
    under a comment reading "only enabled in CI so local `dotnet add package`
    keeps working". No ref in this repository's history has ever contained a
    .github directory or any .yml or .yaml file, so there was no CI for the
    gate to be enabled in: the sentence named a tradeoff between local
    convenience and a CI guarantee where only the convenience ever existed.
    The gate was not dormant pending a workflow, it was never on anywhere,
    and the comment implied otherwise. Nor does the SDK infer the property
    from a CI environment: measured on 10.0.400 in two sibling members, it
    evaluates empty even with CI and GITHUB_ACTIONS set.

    A file whose promise depends on a switch set somewhere else cannot state
    its own promise. Removing the condition makes the promise this file's
    own, which is the reason to prefer it over waiting for a workflow.

    What it buys here, stated precisely because it is less than it sounds.
    The guarantee reaches the package class only: Collatz.Tests' four test
    packages, coverlet.collector, Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk, xunit and
    xunit.runner.visualstudio. It does not reach the BigRational edge, which
    this member takes by ProjectReference. A version arriving that way is
    recorded as a constraint in the consuming project's dependencies map and
    is not validated. Proven rather than inferred: RationalApproximation has
    locked mode live in CI and ran green with a stale preview1 constraint
    against a preview2 producer (halheinrich/Math#44). ContinuedFractions is
    the inverse case; it consumes BigRational as a package, so there the
    change does protect that edge.

    Accepted cost: a deliberate package change now fails the restore with
    NU1004 instead of quietly rewriting the lock. Recover with
    `dotnet restore -p:RestoreForceEvaluate=true`, which is what the CLI's
    force-evaluate switch sets, and commit the regenerated lock. One command,
    once per intended change, on an artefact whose whole purpose is that it
    does not change silently. (The switch is spelled here as a property
    because an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen.) NuGet writes the
    regenerated lock with CRLF while .gitattributes pins it to LF, so
    `git status` then reports a change that `git diff` cannot show.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
